**Q1.3.2.** Let us suppose that a given **discrete ordinal response** `poverty` $Y_i$ (for $i = 1, \dots, n$) has categories `Too Little`, `About Right`, and `Too Much` in a training set of size $n$.

> **Heads-up**: Categories `Too Little`, `About Right`, and `Too Much` implicate an ordinal scale, i.e., `Too Little` $<$ `About Right` $<$ `Too much`

Moreover, $X_{i, \texttt{age}}$ is a continuous regressor along with the following dummy variables:

$$
X_{i, \texttt{Norway}} =
\begin{cases}
1 \; \; \; \; \text{if the subject is from Norway},\\
0 \; \; \; \; \mbox{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
$$

$$
X_{i, \texttt{Sweden}} =
\begin{cases}
1 \; \; \; \; \text{if the subject is from Sweden},\\
0 \; \; \; \; \mbox{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
$$

$$
X_{i, \texttt{USA}} =
\begin{cases}
1 \; \; \; \; \text{if the subject is from USA},\\
0 \; \; \; \; \mbox{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
$$

> **Heads-up:** Note that `Australia` will be the baseline for the variable `country`.

Now, **briefly in plain words**, describe the **two odds** of our chosen modelling framework for the response of interest.

<!--
# BEGIN QUESTION
name: Q1-3-2-reasoning
manual: true
-->

**ANSWER:**

<!-- # BEGIN SOLUTION -->

> \begin{gather*}
\text{Level } \texttt{About Right}  \text{ or any lesser degree versus level } \texttt{Too Much} \\
\text{Level } \texttt{Too Little} \text{ versus level } \texttt{About Right} \text{ or any higher degree}
\end{gather*}

<!-- # END SOLUTION -->

<!-- # END QUESTION -->

**Q1.3.3.** **Mathematically**, what is the system of modelling equations?

<!--
# BEGIN QUESTION
name: Q1-3-3-reasoning
manual: true
-->

**ANSWER:**

<!-- # BEGIN SOLUTION -->

> *We have the following system of $2$ equations:*

> \begin{align*}
\eta_i^{(\texttt{About Right})} &= \log\left[\frac{P(Y_i \leq \texttt{About Right} \mid X_{i, \texttt{age}},X_{i, \texttt{Norway}},X_{i, \texttt{Sweden}},X_{i, \texttt{USA}})}{P(Y_i = \texttt{Too Much} \mid X_{i, \texttt{age}},X_{i, \texttt{Norway}},X_{i, \texttt{Sweden}},X_{i, \texttt{USA}})}\right] \\ &= \beta_0^{(\texttt{About Right})} - \beta_1 X_{i, \texttt{age}} - \beta_2 X_{i, \texttt{Norway}} - \beta_3 X_{i, \texttt{Sweden}} - \beta_4 X_{i, \texttt{USA}}
\end{align*}

> \begin{align*}
\eta_i^{(\texttt{Too Little})} &= \log\left[\frac{P(Y_i = \texttt{Too Little} \mid X_{i, \texttt{age}},X_{i, \texttt{Norway}},X_{i, \texttt{Sweden}},X_{i, \texttt{USA}}}{P(Y_i \geq \texttt{About Right} \mid X_{i, \texttt{age}},X_{i, \texttt{Norway}},X_{i, \texttt{Sweden}},X_{i, \texttt{USA}})}\right] \\ &= \beta_0^{(\texttt{Too Little})} - \beta_1 X_{i, \texttt{age}} - \beta_2 X_{i, \texttt{Norway}} - \beta_3 X_{i, \texttt{Sweden}} - \beta_4 X_{i, \texttt{USA}}.
\end{align*}

> *Then, we can reexpress the previous equations as:*

> \begin{align*}
\frac{P(Y_i = \texttt{Too Much} \mid X_{i, \texttt{age}},X_{i, \texttt{Norway}},X_{i, \texttt{Sweden}},X_{i, \texttt{USA}})}{P(Y_i \leq \texttt{About Right} \mid X_{i, \texttt{age}},X_{i, \texttt{Norway}},X_{i, \texttt{Sweden}},X_{i, \texttt{USA}})} &= \exp\big(-\beta_0^{(\texttt{About Right})}\big) \exp\big(\beta_1 X_{i, \texttt{Age}}\big) \\
& \qquad \exp\big(\beta_2 X_{i, \texttt{Norway}}\big) \exp\big(\beta_3 X_{i, \texttt{Sweden}}\big) \exp\big(\beta_4 X_{i, \texttt{USA}}\big)
\end{align*}

> \begin{align*}
\frac{P(Y_i \geq \texttt{About Right} \mid X_{i, \texttt{age}},X_{i, \texttt{Norway}},X_{i, \texttt{Sweden}},X_{i, \texttt{USA}})}{P(Y_i = \texttt{Too Little} \mid X_{i, \texttt{age}},X_{i, \texttt{Norway}},X_{i, \texttt{Sweden}},X_{i, \texttt{USA}})} &= \exp\big(-\beta_0^{(\texttt{Too Little})}\big) \exp\big(\beta_1 X_{i, \texttt{Age}}\big) \\
& \qquad \exp\big(\beta_2 X_{i, \texttt{Norway}}\big) \exp\big(\beta_3 X_{i, \texttt{Sweden}}\big) \exp\big(\beta_4 X_{i, \texttt{USA}}\big).
\end{align*}

<!-- # END SOLUTION -->

