## Q2.3. Data Modelling Framework

rubric={reasoning:2}

Given the response's nature of this case, **briefly in plain words**, describe the **two odds** that we will attempt to model.

<!--
# BEGIN QUESTION
name: Q2-3-reasoning
manual: true
-->

**ANSWER:**

<!-- # BEGIN SOLUTION -->

> - *The odds that the subject feels `Very happy` versus `Pretty happy` or `Not too happy`.*
> - *The odds that the subject feels `Very happy` or `Pretty happy` versus `Not too happy`.*

<!-- # END SOLUTION -->

<!-- # END QUESTION -->

Now, write out the sample's regression equations for these **two logit functions** under **the proper regression model**. Let $Y_i$ be the happiness level for the $i$th subject. Furthermore, let $X_{i, \texttt{hours}}$ be the number of weekly hours worked by the $i$th subject. Finally, use the following dummy variables:

$$
X_{i, \texttt{Divorced}} =
\begin{cases}
1 \; \; \; \; \mbox{if the $i$th subject is divorced},\\
0 \; \; \; \;   \mbox{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
$$

$$
X_{i, \texttt{Married}} =
\begin{cases}
1 \; \; \; \; \mbox{if the $i$th subject is married},\\
0 \; \; \; \;   \mbox{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
$$

$$
X_{i, \texttt{Separated}} =
\begin{cases}
1 \; \; \; \; \mbox{if the $i$th subject is separated},\\
0 \; \; \; \;   \mbox{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
$$

$$
X_{i, \texttt{Widowed}} =
\begin{cases}
1 \; \; \; \; \mbox{if the $i$th subject is widowed},\\
0 \; \; \; \;   \mbox{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
$$

> **Note:** You do not need to use LaTeX to show your work. That said, you could only embed pictures of your written solution into the `.pdf`. Nevertheless, it is advisable to use LaTeX so you can practice equation writing in reproducible documents. 

<!--
# BEGIN QUESTION
name: Q2-3-reasoning-2
manual: true
-->

**ANSWER:**

<!-- # BEGIN SOLUTION -->

> *We have the following system of $2$ equations:*

> \begin{align*}
\eta_i^{(\texttt{Pretty happy})} &= \log \left[ \frac{P \left( Y_i \leq \texttt{Pretty happy} \mid X_{i, \texttt{hours}}, X_{i, \texttt{Divorced}}, X_{i, \texttt{Married}}, X_{i, \texttt{Separated}}, X_{i, \texttt{Widowed}} \right)}{P \left( Y_i = \texttt{Very happy} \mid X_{i, \texttt{hours}}, X_{i, \texttt{Divorced}}, X_{i, \texttt{Married}}, X_{i, \texttt{Separated}}, X_{i, \texttt{Widowed}} \right)} \right] \\
&= \beta_0^{(\texttt{Pretty happy})} - \beta_1 X_{i, \texttt{hours}} - \beta_2 X_{i, \texttt{Divorced}} - \beta_3 X_{i, \texttt{Married}} - \beta_4 X_{i, \texttt{Separated}} - \beta_5 X_{i, \texttt{Widowed}}.
\end{align*}

> \begin{align*}
\eta_i^{(\texttt{Not too happy})} &= \log \left[ \frac{P \left( Y_i = \texttt{Not too happy} \mid X_{i, \texttt{hours}}, X_{i, \texttt{Divorced}}, X_{i, \texttt{Married}}, X_{i, \texttt{Separated}}, X_{i, \texttt{Widowed}} \right)}{P \left( Y_i \geq \texttt{Pretty happy} \mid X_{i, \texttt{hours}}, X_{i, \texttt{Divorced}}, X_{i, \texttt{Married}}, X_{i, \texttt{Separated}}, X_{i, \texttt{Widowed}} \right)} \right] \\
&= \beta_0^{(\texttt{Not too happy})} - \beta_1 X_{i, \texttt{hours}} - \beta_2 X_{i, \texttt{Divorced}} - \beta_3 X_{i, \texttt{Married}} - \beta_4 X_{i, \texttt{Separated}} - \beta_5 X_{i, \texttt{Widowed}}.
\end{align*}

> *This system is equivalent to:*

> \begin{gather*}
\frac{P(Y_i = \texttt{Very happy} \mid X_{i, \texttt{hours}}, X_{i, \texttt{Divorced}}, X_{i, \texttt{Married}}, X_{i, \texttt{Separated}}, X_{i, \texttt{Widowed}})}{P(Y_i \leq \texttt{Pretty happy} \mid X_{i, \texttt{hours}}, X_{i, \texttt{Divorced}}, X_{i, \texttt{Married}}, X_{i, \texttt{Separated}}, X_{i, \texttt{Widowed}})} = \\ \exp\big(-\beta_0^{(\texttt{Pretty happy})}\big) \exp\big(\beta_1 X_{i, \texttt{hours}}\big) \exp\big(\beta_2 X_{i, \texttt{Divorced}}\big) \exp\big(\beta_3 X_{i, \texttt{Married}}\big) \exp\big(\beta_4 X_{i, \texttt{Separated}}\big) \exp\big(\beta_5 X_{i, \texttt{Widowed}}\big)
\end{gather*}

> \begin{gather*}
\frac{P(Y_i \geq \texttt{Pretty happy} \mid X_{i, \texttt{hours}}, X_{i, \texttt{Divorced}}, X_{i, \texttt{Married}}, X_{i, \texttt{Separated}}, X_{i, \texttt{Widowed}})}{P(Y_i = \texttt{Not too happy} \mid X_{i, \texttt{hours}}, X_{i, \texttt{Divorced}}, X_{i, \texttt{Married}}, X_{i, \texttt{Separated}}, X_{i, \texttt{Widowed}})} = \\ \exp\big(-\beta_0^{(\texttt{Not too happy})}\big) \exp \big(\beta_1 X_{i, \texttt{hours}}\big) \exp\big(\beta_2 X_{i, \texttt{Divorced}}\big) \exp\big(\beta_3 X_{i, \texttt{Married}}\big) \exp\big(\beta_4 X_{i, \texttt{Separated}}\big) \exp\big(\beta_5 X_{i, \texttt{Widowed}}\big).
\end{gather*}


