## Q3.5. Mixed-Effects: Data Modelling Framework

rubric={reasoning:4}

Let us modify our `OLS_fixed_model` so that it becomes a linear mixed-effects (LME) model, ensuring that there is a **mixed intercept by `volunteer`**. Furthermore, consider a **mixed effect for `method`**.

Let $\texttt{time\_to\_sleep}_{i,j}$ be $i$th response for the $j$th `volunteer` with $6$ observations each; where $i = 1, \dots, 6$ and $j = 1, \dots, 16$, and

$$
\texttt{method}_{i,j} =
\begin{cases}
1 \; \; \; \; \mbox{if the method is BBG},\\
0 \; \; \; \;   \mbox{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
$$ 

**Write the sample's mixed-effects model equation.**

<!--
# BEGIN QUESTION
name: Q3-5-reasoning
manual: true
-->

**ANSWER:**

<!-- # BEGIN SOLUTION -->

\begin{align*}
\texttt{time\_to\_sleep}_{i,j}
&= \overbrace{\beta_{0,j}}^{\text{Mixed Effect}} + \overbrace{\beta_{1,j}}^{\text{Mixed Effect}} \times \texttt{method}_{i,j} + \varepsilon_{i,j} \\
&=  (\beta_0 + b_{0,j}) + (\beta_1 + b_{1,j}) \times \texttt{method}_{i,j}  + \varepsilon_{i,j} \\
& \qquad \qquad  \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \text{for} \; i = 1, \ldots, 6 \; \; \text{and} \; \; j = 1, \ldots, 16;
\end{align*}

> *where* $\beta_0$ and $b_{0,j}$ are the fixed and random intercepts respectively, along with $\beta_1$ and $b_{1,j}$ as the fixed and random effects for `method` respectively. The error term $\varepsilon_{i,j}$ has the usual OLS assumptions.

